From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pyodide <pyodide@pyodide.org>
Date: Sun, 30 Mar 2025 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] Remove Py_ALWAYS_INLINE to let LLVM decide inlining

On wasm32 at -Oz, forced inlining of refcount functions (Py_INCREF,
Py_DECREF, etc.) via Py_ALWAYS_INLINE duplicates ~730 KB of code across
all translation units. Removing the forced inlining lets LLVM's cost
model decide per call site -- still inlining in hot paths while keeping
cold paths compact. The wasm JIT (V8 TurboFan / SpiderMonkey Ion)
re-inlines hot call sites at runtime.

See uninlining.md for full analysis.
---
 Include/pyport.h | 19 +++++--------------
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Include/pyport.h b/Include/pyport.h
--- a/Include/pyport.h
+++ b/Include/pyport.h
@@ -325,19 +325,11 @@
 // It must be specified before the function return type. Usage:
 //
 //     static inline Py_ALWAYS_INLINE int random(void) { return 4; }
-#if defined(Py_DEBUG)
-   // If Python is built in debug mode, usually compiler optimizations are
-   // disabled. In this case, Py_ALWAYS_INLINE can increase a lot the stack
-   // memory usage. For example, forcing inlining using gcc -O0 increases the
-   // stack usage from 6 KB to 15 KB per Python function call.
-#  define Py_ALWAYS_INLINE
-#elif defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__) || defined(__INTEL_COMPILER)
-#  define Py_ALWAYS_INLINE __attribute__((always_inline))
-#elif defined(_MSC_VER)
-#  define Py_ALWAYS_INLINE __forceinline
-#else
-#  define Py_ALWAYS_INLINE
-#endif
+// Pyodide: define Py_ALWAYS_INLINE as empty to let LLVM decide inlining
+// at -Oz. Forced always_inline duplicates refcount functions across every
+// translation unit (~730 KB in NumPy alone). The wasm JIT re-inlines hot
+// call sites at runtime. See uninlining.md for full analysis.
+#define Py_ALWAYS_INLINE
 
 // Py_NO_INLINE
 // Disable inlining on a function. For example, it reduces the C stack
